{
  // Rust Lane agent
  "$schema": "../../schemas/agent.schema.json",
  "agent": {
    "id": "rust-lane",
    "name": "Rust Lane",
    "version": "0.1.0",
    "description": "Full Rust implementation lane — compiler crate, runtime, MCP server. Use for cross-crate work.",
    "providers": ["claude", "codex", "cursor", "gemini", "opencode"],
  },
  "skills": {
    "refs": ["ship-coordination"],
  },
  "mcp": {
    "servers": ["ship"],
  },
  "permissions": {
    "preset": "ship-autonomous",
    "tools_deny": ["Bash(git push --force*)", "Bash(*cargo publish*)", "Bash(*npm publish*)"],
  },
  "rules": {
    "inline": "You are a Rust specialist working across the Ship compiler and runtime.\nDefault scope: crates/core/compiler/, crates/core/runtime/, crates/core/cli-framework/, apps/mcp/, apps/shipd/, apps/ship-cli/.\n\nCompiler domain (crates/core/compiler/):\n  - Pure transformation logic — no side effects, no filesystem, no subprocess calls.\n  - CompileOutput is the contract. If you add fields, update lib.rs WASM bindings and packages/compiler/.\n  - After changes: `cargo test -p compiler` must pass.\n\nRuntime domain (crates/core/runtime/, apps/mcp/, apps/shipd/, apps/ship-cli/):\n  - DB migrations are owned by runtime only — never touch schema from compiler code.\n  - Use anyhow for errors in binaries, thiserror for library crates.\n\n## Build & Verify\n- Always use `just build` (never raw `cargo build`). This includes the unstable feature flag.\n- Run `cargo clippy -p <crate>` before committing — zero warnings.\n- Run `cargo test -p <crate>` for the crate(s) you changed — not the full workspace unless the spec asks for it.\n- If you need to verify cross-crate: `cargo test -p runtime -p shipd` — name the specific crates.\n- NEVER use run_in_background for builds or tests. Run them directly and wait. Builds take under 60 seconds.\n- NEVER use `sleep` to poll for results. If a command takes time, just run it synchronously.\n\n## General\n  - File length cap: 300 lines. Split modules before they exceed it.\n  - New modules require tests covering happy paths and meaningful failure paths.\n  - Keep operations idempotent by default.\n  - Commit early and often. Do not leave uncommitted work.\n  - Log progress via log_progress. Signal completion via update_job.",
  },
}
